Paper summary
This paper proposes RAN-NFM, a multimodal foundation model framework for cellular RAN profiling and behavior prediction. The framework consists of RAN-NFM-Base (self-supervised, task-agnostic pretraining using SAINT for tabular data and PatchTST for time-series, fused via cross-attention), RAN-NFM-Core (lightweight fine-tuning without LLM), and RAN-NFM-Pro (incorporating LLM-derived features via LoRA). The model is pretrained on 561M samples from 166K sectors of a tier-1 U.S. provider's LTE network. The evaluation spans two use cases, i.e., configuration tuning (classification) and energy-saving throughput prediction (regression). RAN-NFM-Core achieves 8.6% AUPRC improvement over the production baseline while being 168x more memory-efficient than LLM-based alternatives, and RAN-NFM-Pro achieves the best accuracy overall.
Reasons to accept
No prior has implemented a system combining tabular configuration data, time-series KPIs, and optionally LLM-derived features into a unified foundation model for RAN cell profiling. Existing telecom foundation models are either text-only (TelecomGPT), single-modality (Large Wireless Model for RF data), or address different networking domains entirely (NetLLM targets video streaming and job scheduling). The gap is explicitly identified in recent survey papers on telecom foundation models (2024-2025) as an open challenge, thus this paper fills a clear gap.

The decision to use specialized encoders (SAINT for tabular, PatchTST for time-series) rather than forcing all data through an LLM is well-supported by recent evidence (NeurIPS 2024 spotlight paper [25]) showing LLMs perform poorly on regression-based time-series forecasting (though there are techniques like Chronos - cited in the paper as reference [1] - that do this effectively while restricted to univariate time series) The Core vs. Pro dichotomy (168x memory reduction with competitive accuracy) is a practically useful design point. The cross-attention fusion using linear attention [14] to control computational cost is a sensible engineering choice.

The paper demonstrates deployment with a tier-1 U.S. service provider. The field deployment results (Section 5.5) show that RAN-NFM-Pro consistently delivers the highest throughput gains across bottom-20% and mid-60% sectors compared to the Auric XGB baseline, which is a very strong outcome.

The what-if analysis capability, i.e., predicting how the RAN would behave if a configuration change or outage occurs addresses a real operational need. The Event Injection Fine-Tuning (EIFT) design (Section 3.4), which injects event embeddings during fine-tuning via a dedicated encoder branch, is clean and the ablation study (Figure 6c) shows it substantially outperforms both vanilla MLP and partial-head alternatives.

Pretraining on 561M samples from 166K sectors over 6 months of real operational data is really solid. The feature set (Table 5) is comprehensive and reflects operator knowledge of what matters for RAN profiling.

Reasons not to accept
A hallmark of foundation models is their ability to generalize across tasks, ideally with few-shot or zero-shot transfer. RAN-NFM is evaluated on only two downstream tasks, both of which predict throughput (one as classification, one as regression). This is insufficient to support the claim of "a wide range of downstream tasks" (Section 1, contribution 2). True foundation model evaluation would demonstrate transfer to genuinely diverse tasks, e.g., latency, jitter, mobility management, or capacity planning. Without this, RAN-NFM is better characterized as a multi-modal pretrained model with task-specific fine-tuning, not a foundation model in the established sense.

The entire evaluation uses data from a single tier-1 U.S. LTE provider. While this is good, Section 7 ("Adaptability Cross RAN Technology and Service Providers") claims the model can adapt to 5G SA, future 6G, and different providers, but it does not provide any evidence. This gap should be closed: LTE and 5G NR have fundamentally different RAN parameters, KPI definitions, and radio behaviors.

For RANPerf, the best AUPRC is 0.286 (RAN-NFM-Pro, Table 1) and the best F1 is 0.318 which are low in absolute terms. From the PR curve (Figure 6a), at 20% recall RAN-NFM-Pro achieves roughly 50% precision which means that half of all recommended configuration changes would fail to produce the promised 30% throughput improvement. While improvements over the baseline are real, one might question whether these models are accurate enough for production-grade automated decision-making. The paper would benefit from a discussion on the absolute performance ceiling and what limits it.

The paper motivates the architecture by arguing that "LLM-only adaptation performs poorly on regression-based time-series forecasting" (Section 1). Yet RAN-NFM-Pro which incorporates an LLM branch outperforms RAN-NFM-Core consistently. For EngSav (the regression task), RAN-NFM-Pro achieves 14% lower MAPE than the baseline while RAN-NFM-Core achieves only 6% lower MAPE. The paper should clarify this contraddiction: is the LLM contributing through improved representation learning rather than direct regression? What exactly does the LLM branch learn that cross-attention alone cannot capture?

The RANPerf field deployment results (Section 5.5.1) reports mean delta throughput across sector groups but provides no confidence intervals or any other significance testing. If a bottom-20% sector improves, was it because of the model's recommendation or would it have improved anyway? For EngSav (Section 5.5.2), the reported metric (MAD vs. the baseline policy) measures divergence from the prior production solution, not actual energy savings or throughput impact. To be a meaningful measure of model quality, one would need to see actual energy savings and measured throughput.

Other unclear aspects. (a) The cross-attention design uses time-series as query and tabular as key/value (Section 3.2.2). Why this direction? An ablation swapping query/key assignments would clarify benefits of this decision. (b) General-purpose time-series foundation models (Chronos [1], TimesFM) are not compared against. Only encoder-substitution experiments (TPBERTa, TTMs, TimeMixer) are reported, and these perform extremely poorly (TPB-TTMs: AUPRC 0.0439), suggesting they may not have been properly tuned or adapted. (c) No comparison with traditional multi-task learning approaches that share a backbone but train multiple task heads jointly.

Comments for authors
Thank you for submitting to SIGCOMM. I appreciate the work around RAN-NFM. Making RAN managment easier and automatizable is a problem that the community has looked into for long time, and RAN-NFM is another step into realizing this vision. That said, there are several areas that need further clarification.

Suggested Changes based on Weaknesses

Evaluation:

-- Even a small-scale experiment on 5G NR data or data from a second provider would substantially strengthen Section 7's claims. If the data is unavailable, at least acknowledge this as a limitation rather than presenting unsubstantiated generalization claims.

-- TPB-TTMs achieving 0.0439 AUPRC (barely above random) suggests a configuration or adaptation problem rather than a fundamental limitation of those encoders. This weakens the ablation study's conclusions. Please report how these models were adapted and whether hyperparameter search was conducted.

Discussion

-- Two tasks is not enough to justify "foundation model" claims. Adding 2-3 more diverse tasks and demonstrating few-shot adaptation with limited labeled data per task would significantly improve the paper. If this is infeasible, reframe the contribution as a "multi-modal pretrained model for RAN throughput prediction" rather than a general-purpose foundation model.

-- An AUPRC of 0.286 and F1 of 0.318 suggest this is an inherently difficult task. Is this because the 30% threshold is too aggressive? Because the feature set is insufficient (e.g., missing interference information from neighboring cells, user mobility patterns, spectrum conditions)? Or because the positive class is too rare? Understanding the limiting factors matters more than incrementally improving over a weak baseline.

Minor Issues

Section 4.2, paragraph on RANPerf: "This task is build on top" should be "This task is built on top."
Section 5.1: The StatF-MLP baseline description is duplicated (paragraphs 2 and 3 under "Baselines Solutions" describe it identically).
Table 5: The feature ARPPRIORITYLEVELFORSPIFHO appears twice in the configuration features list (under "Load Steering Parameters" and "Mobility Parameters") with the same description.
Section 8 (Conclusion): "RAN-NFM-Core, a multi-model RAN foundation model" -- "multi-model" should be "multi-modal." Also, "adaption" should be "adaptation" (appears twice).
The paper claims 34 features total (21 config + 5 categorical + 8 KPI) but Table 5 lists only 7 KPI features under "KPI Time-Series Features" (the 8th, DrbThpVolQCI6to9, appears to be the target variable in some tasks).
Clarification Questions for Authors

How does the model handle temporal distribution shift? RAN configurations and traffic patterns change over time (e.g., new spectrum deployments, subscriber growth). Has any analysis been done on model staleness?
What is the positive-class ratio for the RANPerf task? The PR curve and low AUPRC suggest extreme class imbalance. How was this addressed during training?
For the energy-saving use case, how are ground-truth labels obtained? The paper mentions "historical real-world cell-sleeping scenarios." Does this mean only sectors that have already been put to sleep are included? If so, this creates selection bias.
Why was the pretraining objective limited to masked time-series reconstruction? Did you experiment with joint reconstruction of tabular features, or contrastive objectives?
