Paper summary
RAN-NFM introduces a multimodal foundation-model framework for cellular RAN profiling, aiming to replace task-specific models that cannot generalize across diverse operational tasks. The authors design RAN-NFM-Base, a self-supervised backbone that jointly learns tabular RAN configuration features and KPI time-series dynamics via specialized encoders and cross-modal attention, enabling reusable, task-agnostic representations. Two adaptation variants are built on top: RAN-NFM-Core, a lightweight cross-attention–only model for cost-sensitive deployments, and RAN-NFM-Pro, which further incorporates an LLM branch for maximum accuracy. Using six months of real 2025 data from a cellular network operator, the framework is evaluated on two practical tasks—configuration-change impact prediction (RANPerf) and throughput forecasting for energy-saving cell-sleeping (EngSav). Experiment results show that RAN-NFM outperforms production baselines.
Reasons to accept
The paper introduces a domain-tailored architecture that effectively captures relationships between RAN configuration data and KPI time-series, outperforming generic encoders and LLM-only baselines.

Experiment results show that RAN-NFM outperforms production baselines. The models have been successfully deployed for 6+ months in a tier-1 U.S. operator’s live network.

Reasons not to accept
Key technical contribution seem unclear or weak. It assembles a few well-known techniques in AI.

Although this paper motivates the necessity of a multi-modal foundation model in RAN, the evaluation of its generalizability is quite limited. It only tests in two use cases.

Comments for authors
This paper presents a framework for multimodal RAN foundation modeling. The proposed architecture aligns naturally with the heterogeneous structure of RAN data and achieves strong gains over both production systems and generic encoder baselines. The large-scale self-supervised pretraining on operational samples provides strong generalization.

However, several areas could be strengthened:

Although this paper motivates the necessity of a multi-modal foundation model in RAN, the evaluation of its generalizability is quite limited. It only tests in two use cases. It is unclear if the foundation model can support multiple downstream applications as claimed.

The motivation can be better clarified. The discussion of prior work, especially NetLLM, is limited. The paper states that LLM-only adaptation performs poorly on regression-based RAN tasks but does not explain why or whether improved LLM adaptation strategies could close the gap, leaving unclear whether a new domain-specific foundation model is truly necessary. If true, a follow-up question is, why would time-series foundation models not transfer in the RAN setting, and what properties of RAN data or objectives prevent those approaches from being effective? Clarifying this would strengthen the argument that RAN-specific multimodal pretraining is uniquely necessary rather than one possible design choice among several competitive alternatives.

The problem formulation is not clear. The paper does not clearly delineate RAN management tasks. In the absence of this contextual grounding, readers lack a clear understanding of the practical motivations behind the proposed design choices. Consequently, it becomes difficult to assess whether the model architecture and training strategy are genuinely aligned with real-world operational requirements. Providing a structured overview of typical RAN workflows, decision-making processes, and data modalities would substantially strengthen the paper’s claims regarding applicability and practical relevance.

The technical contributions are not well presented. Most architectural components are adopted directly from prior work. As a result, this work seems to be an engineering-driven integration with limited technical contributions. Besides, the role and necessity of the LLM-based adaptation branch remain unclear (e.g., why this integration enhances feature learning). The conceptual justification behind these design choices could be strengthened.

Writing and presentation issues. Several figures could be resized or reformatted for better readability, and the evaluation section, given its complexity, would benefit from an introductory overview at the beginning that explains the purpose of each experimental component and how each supports the paper’s main claims.

The evaluation is based on real-world dataset, which is remarkable. Yet, there is not much insight into the dataset or the unique challenges of studied problem. No dataset is publically available for reproducible evaluation or validation.

