Paper summary
In this presented work, the authors proposed a multimodal foundation model framework for Radio Access Network (RAN) profiling and prediction tasks named RAN-NFM. The key motivation behind this design is that existing RAN optimization solutions rely on either rule-based heuristics or task-specific machine learning models that do not generalize across multiple operational tasks. In contrast, the proposed RAN-NFM model learns reusable representations from heterogeneous RAN data sources. Specifically, five interdependent modules were designed, i.e., SAINT tabular encoder for configuration data, PatchTST time-series encoder for KPI dynamics, cross-modal attention fusion to capture dependencies between modalities, EIFT (Event Injection Fine-Tuning) for what-if prediction tasks involving configuration changes, and the optional LLM module was adopted to enhance semantic representations. The authors further evaluate the performance of their proposed RAN-NFM model by using six months of real-world data from a tier-1 U.S. cellular provider, containing 166K sectors and 561M samples.
Reasons to accept
The design is well-motivated since the problem considered in this work, i.e., applying foundation models to network operations and RAN optimization, is timely and relevant, especially as networks generate increasingly large volumes of heterogeneous data.

The proposed foundation model enables adaptation to multiple downstream tasks (e.g., classification, regression, etc.) using lightweight fine-tuning.

The evaluation adopted large-scale real-world data.
Reasons not to accept
While the paper introduces a foundation model architecture, the core technical contributions primarily involve adaptation of existing ML architectures (SAINT, PatchTST, LoRA-based LLM adaptation). Accordingly, the novelty lies more in system integration.
The evaluation mainly focused on ML baselines and prior learning-based approaches. The authors did not compare their work to other types of methods, such as control-theory-based network management solutions, deep Reinforcement Learning-based RAN optimization frameworks, etc.
As mentioned in the paper, the proposed RAN-NFM model requires significant computing resources.
Additionally, the authors did not test the generalizability and the robustness of the proposed RAN-NFM. For example, as network conditions evolve, how will the proposed method perform? How sensitive is the model to noisy or missing KPI measurements?
