Paper summary
The paper proposes RAN-NFM, a multimodal foundation-model framework for cellular RAN management that combines sector configuration/property features with KPI time-series telemetry. It pretrains a shared backbone using self-supervised reconstruction of masked KPI sequences, then adapts the learned representation to downstream operational tasks via fine-tuning. To support "what-if" style queries tied to network actions, it introduces an event-conditioning fine-tuning mechanism and offers both a lightweight core model and a heavier variant augmented with language-model embeddings. The evaluation focuses on predicting performance gains from configuration changes and forecasting throughput under energy-saving cell sleeping scenarios.
Reasons to accept
It tackles an important problem of RAN configuration management with a foundation model-based approach.
It shows evaluation results from field deployment
Reasons not to accept
Evidence for broad "task generalization" is limited by the chosen use cases.
As the approach (FM + finetuning) is general, it is unclear whether there are unique challenges in the context of RAN management. The paper does not describe them clearly.
Comments for authors
Thanks for submitting your paper. The paper leverages large-scale RAN telemetry to build reusable models for performance and energy-related workflows and presents a coherent multimodal pipeline (property/config + KPI time series) with pretraining and downstream adaptation. While I like the high-level approach, several of the challenges and corresponding solutions feel less novel. Below are my comments on the ideas and questions.

The paper motivates a foundation model as a way to generalize across diverse downstream RAN tasks. However, the main evaluated use cases are both throughput-centric and likely share similar signal sources and temporal structure. This makes it difficult to evaluate whether the approach truly generalizes beyond a narrow family of KPI prediction problems. Can you demonstrate transfer to at least one qualitatively different RAN task (e.g., mobility/handover anomalies, interference issues, KPI root-cause classification, paging/registration instability)? If additional tasks cannot be shown, can you scope the claim more clearly to throughput-related tasks rather than broad RAN FM generalization?

Regarding the approach of fine-tuning the base model, it is unclear what is technically new here beyond adopting LoRA and offering two model sizes. Are there any unique challenges in the context of RAN management? What is the concrete operating scenario in which the Pro variant is expected to run? What retraining cadence and serving architecture are assumed for Pro? Can you provide a clearer cost/benefit justification showing Pro is needed and practically deployable (and when Core is insufficient)?

Using cross-attention to fuse time-series embeddings with tabular/context features is a common design in multimodal forecasting and representation learning. While it may work well, it is not obvious what is uniquely RAN-specific about this mechanism. Do you have ablations against simpler multimodal fusion strategies, and can you explain what specific RAN property of the data makes cross-attention essential or challenging? What aspects of operator telemetry (e.g., sparse categorical configs, long-term indicators, nonstationarity) are you leveraging that prior multimodal forecasters do not?

The paper highlights data quality and imbalance as a challenge in operational RAN telemetry, but the solution appears primarily to rely on scale and self-supervised pretraining, along with task-specific labeling thresholds. This is reasonable, but it is a fairly generic answer unless the paper provides concrete methods for handling imbalance/noise (e.g., sampling/weighting, calibration, robust losses, label-noise mitigation) and demonstrates that they matter. What specific data issues (missingness, noisy KPIs, shift across software versions, label imbalance) are most problematic in your datasets, and what specific mechanisms in your training pipeline address them? Can you quantify robustness improvements attributable to these choices (e.g., under severe imbalance or known noise regimes)?

