Paper summary
This paper describes RAN-NFM, a foundation model built to be able to generate cellular network configurations. Working with a national ("tier-1") operator, the authors gather lots of configuration and associated performance measurement data and generate said model with a clever selection of encodings suited to the task. The system is evaluated through a number of mechanisms, including an accuracy analysis, efficiency analysis, and a somewhat underspecified "field deployment" where it outperforms the state of the art.
Reasons to accept
Impressive engineering effort with partnerships and system building Very thorough evaluation with only a few missing elements Great description of the solution space and comparison across alternatives.
Reasons not to accept
A bit of the "here's a giant pool of data, we made a model" style of ML/Systems work going around; would be good to have an ablation study on the configuration variables themselves Evaluation generally good but the field study is underspecified; it's not clear if this was run in prod or through some other evaluation (I am assuming the latter) A lot of the figures are really hard to interpret.
Comments for authors
Thanks for the submission! While I'm positive, I do have some concerns. On the positive side, there's been a few of these ML-for-cellular-config papers in my stack at the last few venues and this seems to me to be the strongest; the assumptions are limited and the data source seems solid. I am convinced the system works as described and provides a benefit.

On the negative side, I have two main issues. First, while the overall engineering is impressive it's not really clear what's important here from the configuration side; generally these systems find a few critical variables to tweak and much of the remaining has relatively little impact. Given the large number of configuration variables involved, that seems likely the case here. This could mean that the partner's network could be better served through a much simpler optimization function on a smaller number of variables or that the existing system is suboptimally tuned for some reason outside of the researcher understanding. This is supported by the power study where we learned that history was a bad predictor in that case. A light ablation study, potentially informed by the actual configuration changes from the field study, would be a good exploration. It's hard to truly believe that the whole model is that important. I'm not even sure why we chose these variables to begin with.

Second, the "field study" is pretty wildly underspecified; it's not clear if the system as "deployed in production for more than six months" means it was actually changing production configurations and seeing measured changes or if we're doing predictions on a production network. If it's the former, that's really impressive. We just need more background on the deployment; rough location, number of variables changed, etc.

Lastly, the figures are rough; 6b for example seems to give a light indication of improved performance but the degree is unclear. Same with most of 7. You might consider pushing a few to the appendix and focusing on the most important results.

Some smaller issues: The sentence ""The results from both use cases demonstrate that these single modality, general purpose tabular and time-series encoders are not optimal for the RAN profiling task." is repeated
